WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.560
ACQUISITIONS GALORE:
Ever since Satya Nadella took over Microsoft

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:08.540
in early 2014, Microsoft has become the king
of acquisitions.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.570
They basically buy everything in sight in
every sector you can think of, and they’ve

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.090
only gotten more and more risk on with time.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.489
Mojang $2.5 billion.

00:00:18.489 --> 00:00:20.670
LinkedIn $26.2 billion.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.180
GitHub $7.5 billion.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.570
ZeniMax $7.5 billion.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.539
Nuance Communications $19.7 billion.

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:33.540
And most recently, Activitision Blizzard for
$68.7 billion.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.320
And those are just the most notable acquisitions.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.989
Microsoft has completed dozens upon dozens
of smallers acquisitions throughout the same

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:42.219
time period.

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:45.350
And let’s not forget about their investments
either.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:50.620
Just last year, Microsoft agreed to invest
$10 billion into OpenAI.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:56.070
As you can see, Microsoft has committed well
over a $140 billion into investments and acquisitions

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.750
over the past decade.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.539
For perspective, Meta has spent roughly $25
billion on acquisitions through the same time

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:03.539
period.

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:09.820
Google has spent $16 billion, Amazon has spent
$30 billion, and Apple has only spent $6 billion.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.970
In other words, Microsoft has nearly spent
more on acquisitions than all of their peers

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.540
combined times 2.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.140
Their investments alone are literally enough
to be a Fortune 100 company itself.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.900
Clearly, a gutsy move but this has often been
described as being the smart play.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.400
It’s not like their peers aren’t investing
similar amounts of capital, it’s just that

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.340
they don’t invest as much into acquisitions
when maybe they should be.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.659
For obvious reasons, buying highly established
multi deca billion companies is a lot safer

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:44.660
than betting a $100 billion on the Metaverse.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.560
But, is it really?

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.700
Microsoft’s acquisitions have obviously
played out in their favor over the past decade

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.210
but that was during an everlasting bull market
where everything tech related was going up.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.000
During downturns, however, having clear focus
on a few sectors and pinpoint precision over

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.040
your subsidiaries is critical.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.780
And Microsoft’s massive holdings is basically
the exact opposite of that.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.940
So, will Microsoft’s acquisition galore
eventually end up backfiring?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:21.140
A CAUTIONARY TALE:
Likely the best example of a company failing

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.890
because it was too bloated is none other than
GE.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.580
If you’re not familiar with GE, they were
an industrial giant that was once the largest

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.270
company in the world throughout much of the
2000s.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:37.610
It was created by Thomas Edison in 1892 and
over the next century, they became involved

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.010
in everything under the sun.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.130
From manufacturing kitchen appliances and
household electronics to building jet engines

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.890
and handing out loans.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.640
And this evolution was largely thanks to a
man named Jack Welch or Neutron Jack.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.780
Jack was a shrewd businessman that was basically
the embodiment of all of the worst parts of

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.840
capitalism.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.440
For example, he was the creator of the infamous
rank and yank policy.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.510
This is when you fire the bottom 10% of your
workforce every single year in order to constantly

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.599
keep everyone one their feet and progressing.

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:14.800
And while that might sound like a decent strategy
on paper, it usually just leads to a bunch

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.980
infighting, backstabbing, and office politics
instead of true forward progress.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.680
GE leveraged this strategy for a full 20 years
between 1981 and 2001.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.770
This is one of the key strategies that got
them to the top of the Fortune 500, and it

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:35.129
wasn’t till the 2010s that the effects of
this practice really started to shine through.

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:39.909
The reality was that GE’s workforce had
just become a soulless environment where people

00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:41.299
only looked out for themselves.

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:46.769
As such, when the company started falling,
most of their employees just jumped ship and

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:52.240
the ones that remained didn’t really unite
together, making the crash even more painful.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.189
And you know why this practice is especially
relevant?

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:58.780
Well, Microsoft leveraged the same strategy
under Steve Ballmer’s leadership between

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.060
2000 and 2014.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.849
And that was just the most notable example.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:07.849
Steve very much looked up to Jack Welch and
tried to emulate his management style as much

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:13.770
as possible, leading to ominously similar
workforces.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:18.690
Within the tech world, Microsoft has been
somewhat of a dark horse ever since the 2000s.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.090
They’ve grown from hundreds of billions
of dollars to trillions of dollars while scaling

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:25.340
their workforce from tens of thousands to
hundreds of thousands.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.240
But despite this insane growth, no one is
particularly excited about Microsoft, I mean

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.380
other than Steve Ballmer, but let’s just
say he’s biased.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.930
Seriously though, when was the last time that
people were truly excited about a Microsoft

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:39.930
product?

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:45.640
Their partnership with ChatGPT was likely
the highlight of their last 24 years of business.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.880
Conversely, Apple is able to reintroduce the
iPhone 14 with a new name and garner 37 million

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.330
views on YouTube alone.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.410
It’s the same thing with even Google and
Facebook.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.240
While these companies get loads of criticism,
people still pay attention to them.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.300
Just recently, Threads was able to surpass
100 million downloads right after launch.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.590
With Microsoft on the other hand, even people
who are usually in the know often don’t

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:12.460
know that they own GitHub or LinkedIn.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.150
The only other tech giant who’s workforce
is similar in nature is Amazon which is not

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.690
surprising given that Jeff Bezos is also a
fan of Jack Welch.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:25.000
In fact, they still do rank and yanking to
this day.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.050
But anyway, whether it be consumers, employees,
or even investors, dealings with Microsoft

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.520
are largely just transactional.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.580
You could say that Microsoft is basically
the definition of soulless corporation.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.270
This has been great for flying under the radar,
minimizing press coverage, and yeeting regulation.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:49.440
But, this also brings into question how well
the company can assemble and unite during

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.440
a downturn.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.180
If GE was anything to go by, it’s likely
that Microsoft’s employees, leadership,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.410
and investors would also just bail as soon
as things got tough.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:03.280
In fact, they’d probably be praised by the
friends and family and even the news for seeing

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.849
the writing on the wall and jumping ship before
it was too late.

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:13.090
But, soulessness is only the first similarity
between Microsoft and GE.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.550
If we look behind the scenes, things get quite
unsettling.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:25.009
UNSETTLING SIMILARITIES:
There’s hundreds and hundreds of articles

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:31.349
and case studies out there about why GE failed
but I don’t think any of them are as relevant

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:34.990
as the case study written by Bill Gates himself.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:38.250
And honestly, his article is quite unnerving.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:42.220
I feel that you could go through the entire
piece and replace every mention of GE with

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.550
Microsoft and it would fit perfectly.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:50.620
For example, Gates could’ve said “Microsoft’s
fall is not the result of innovators developing

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.099
a better OS or cloud infrastructure.

00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:56.169
It’s also not a case of outright fraud,
like Enron.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:01.630
It’s a textbook case of mismanagement of
an overly complex business.”

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.970
“Only a few people saw it coming.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:08.690
I wish I could tell you that I was one of
them, but I was just as surprised as most

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:09.690
people.”

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:14.620
“That’s why I was eager to read Lights
Out: Pride, Delusion, and the Fall of Microsoft.”

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:18.950
“Gryta and Mann gave me the detailed insight
I was looking for into the culture, decisions,

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:23.490
and accounting that eventually caught up to
Microsoft in a gigantic way.”

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.020
As you can see, GE didn’t fall because of
some sort of monumental event like fraud or

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:31.500
being out innovated or even the 2008 financial
crisis.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:37.039
Instead, they slowly rotted from the inside
out, something that no one really spotted

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:38.860
until it was too late.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:44.350
But looking back, 2 red flags regarding GE’s
downfall become apparent in retrospect starting

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.539
with how GE’s management team always made
the numbers.

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:53.560
Quarter after quarter and year after year,
GE consistently posted increasing revenue

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:58.110
and profit just like Microsoft has since Satya
tookover.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.581
Growth in itself is not a bad thing but such
consistent growth can often be a red flag

00:08:02.581 --> 00:08:05.120
for more nefarious activities.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:09.860
Not necessarily illegal activites per se but
activities that ensure growth no matter the

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:10.860
cost.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.940
GE for example, would often sell assets to
friends in order to boost quarterly profits.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.400
Bill Gates of course goes on to claim that
Microsoft would never do anything to artificially

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.550
boost profits, but I’m not sure that we
can necessarily take him at his word.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:30.240
Last year for example, Microsoft skipped salary
increases despite high inflation due to quote

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.469
on quote economic uncertainties.

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:38.250
Now, this would be completely understandable
if Microsoft was at breakeven or burning money.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:44.769
But Microsoft would actually go on to post
record profits of $77 billion that same year.

00:08:44.769 --> 00:08:49.029
This means that the only reason Microsoft
didn’t go through with salary increases

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:51.300
was to make Wall Street happy.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:55.890
To meet the numbers no matter the cost and
put on a success theater despite the economic

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.930
headwinds just like GE.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:04.200
Microsoft wants to be the company that performs
no matter the situation when in reality, that’s

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:08.480
simply not naturally possible leading to questionable
choices.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.870
This brings us into Bill Gates’ second takeaway
about GE which again can largely be applied

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.450
to Microsoft.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:19.769
My second big takeaway from Lights Out is
that Microsoft didn’t have the right talent

00:09:19.769 --> 00:09:24.750
and systems to bundle together a dizzying
array of unrelated businesses—including

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:30.320
cloud infrastructure, social media, AI, tech
hardware, gaming, video conferencing, and

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.670
consumer software —and manage them well.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.040
Investors bought into the notion that the
company’s hands off management style was

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.710
better at managing things than anyone else,
and that Microsoft could produce consistent

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.010
profits even in highly cyclical markets.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:49.079
And Microsoft successfully persuaded people
that its empathetic leader Satya could avoid

00:09:49.079 --> 00:09:52.660
the pitfalls that had tripped up big conglomerates
in the past.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.810
In reality, Satya often didn’t understand
the specifics of the industries they had to

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.290
manage and couldn’t navigate trends in their
industries.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:04.820
For example, the authors make the case that
VP Phil Spencer didn’t have a good handle

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:09.810
on its huge gaming unit, Microsoft Gaming
which gambled tens of billions on acquisitions,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:14.329
sold money losing consoles, and wasn’t even
close to Nintendo or Sony.

00:10:14.329 --> 00:10:19.250
“Making money from [Microsoft Gaming] seemed
shockingly simple to him at first, as they

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:25.930
had already done it with Minecraft, but consistently
satisfying a wide range of gamers was treacherously

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:31.089
complex, and deep risks lurked there and were
not always easily spotted in the quarterly

00:10:31.089 --> 00:10:33.130
profits and losses”.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.950
One former Microsoft executive told the authors
that “Spencer struggled with basic concepts—the

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:42.370
difference between the needs of console and
mobile gamers, for instance, which was fundamental

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:45.370
to a lending operation like Microsoft Gaming.”

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.970
This dynamic was not confined to Spencer.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.170
It was rampant throughout the company’s
top ranks.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:54.889
As a result, the ability of top executives
to understand what was really going on was

00:10:54.889 --> 00:10:56.240
quite limited.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.190
The only people who could actually dig down
into the numbers and see what was going on

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:01.700
were in finance.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:05.800
And as I mentioned above, the finance people
didn’t have much incentive to bring negative

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.470
news to Satya or Spencer.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:14.010
As you can see, the parallels between GE and
Microsoft are stunning which brings us into

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:18.330
the question of what does this all mean.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:25.640
IS THIS TIME DIFFERENT?:
As most of you probably know, we’ve mainly

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.320
covered Microsoft from a positive light over
the past year.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:33.290
And in the end, that is still the prevailing
conlusion.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.620
Microsoft is still killing it and though these
shortfalls may eventually catch up with them,

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:42.519
it’s not exactly the greatest idea to bet
on that because you never know how long that

00:11:42.519 --> 00:11:44.620
could take to play out.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:48.690
After all, GE was on top for decades before
they crumbled.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:52.750
So, Microsoft could easily continue growing
for another decade before cracks start to

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:53.790
show.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:59.210
And in the investing world, the trend is always
your friend until it’s not.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:04.440
But, this does bring up the question of whether
this is how Microsoft will eventually fade

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:05.440
away.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.649
In the past, we’ve seen massive anti trust
lawsuits, terrible consumer sentiment, and

00:12:10.649 --> 00:12:13.570
over a decade of stagnation.

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:19.649
But maybe in the end, it will just be Microsoft’s
own raw weight that sinks them into the ground.

00:12:19.649 --> 00:12:23.339
Of course, the prevailing theory is that given
Microsoft’s hands off management style,

00:12:23.339 --> 00:12:27.190
they’re subsidiaries are rather quick and
nimble in their own right thus making the

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.870
entire company quick and nimble.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:35.180
But, even if that is the case today and Satya
truly is a super star, will this always be

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.050
the case with Microsoft?

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:42.440
Will they always be one step ahead of the
pitfalls of massive bloated corporations or

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:48.010
has Microsoft just spent $141 billion on a
ticking time bomb.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.389
Only time will tell.

00:12:50.389 --> 00:12:54.260
Over the next decade, Microsoft will not only
have to step up to the plate in regards to

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:59.190
management but they’ll also have to address
the concern of market saturation just like

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.510
all of their tech peers.

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:06.080
Check out this video to learn more.

